From: n5 worker <ai@wiltaylor.dev>
Subject: [PATCH] vkr: do not create queue-sync fences exportable

vkr_device_alloc_queue_sync() created every queue-sync fence with
VkExportFenceCreateInfo (SYNC_FD) when the device has
VK_KHR_external_fence_fd. Nothing exports these fences: the queue's sync
thread only waits on them with vkWaitForFences and then retires the virtio
fence. On NVIDIA's Linux driver (610.57.04, RTX 5090) a wait on an
exportable fence wakes up to about 10 ms after the fence signals; a plain
fence wakes within about 0.3 ms. Every ring-indexed context fence of a
Venus context was therefore about 10 ms late on NVIDIA hosts, and a guest
that waits on one per frame (a Windows guest's D3D9 Blt present waits on
two) ran at about 40 fps.

Evidence: vmlab-win3d docs/n5.md, docs/evidence/n5/.
---
--- a/src/venus/vkr_queue.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_queue.c
@@ -28,13 +28,13 @@
       if (!sync)
          return NULL;
 
-      const VkExportFenceCreateInfo export_info = {
-         .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_EXPORT_FENCE_CREATE_INFO,
-         .handleTypes = VK_EXTERNAL_FENCE_HANDLE_TYPE_SYNC_FD_BIT,
-      };
+      /* The sync thread only waits on this fence; nothing exports it.
+       * Creating it exportable (SYNC_FD) costs up to 10 ms per wake-up in
+       * vkWaitForFences on NVIDIA's Linux driver (610.57), which delays every
+       * ring fence by that much.
+       */
       const struct VkFenceCreateInfo create_info = {
          .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_FENCE_CREATE_INFO,
-         .pNext = dev->physical_device->KHR_external_fence_fd ? &export_info : NULL,
       };
       VkResult result =
          vk->CreateFence(dev->base.handle.device, &create_info, NULL, &sync->fence);
